      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-heading font-bold text-text-primary">
        The Enterprise Performance Management Wheel of Fortune
      </h1>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 max-w-3xl prose-archive">
      <p>I'm extremely proud to announce I'll be presenting at ODTUG's Kscope16 in Chicago in July of 2016.  For details on the event, <a href="http://kscope16.com">click here</a>.</p>

<p>One of my presentations is something new this year: the EPM Wheel of Fortune.</p>

<img class="alignnone size-full wp-image-207" src="/images/archive/wheel.jpg" alt="wheel" width="600" height="728" />

<p>Here's how it works:<br />
<ul><br />
	<li>An audience member volunteers to spin the wheel (yes, it's literally a wheel that spins), and it will land on a key driver of value in many organizations;</li><br />
	<li>Then, together with the audience, we agree on the definition of the driver and we decompose it into some of its component parts;</li><br />
	<li>We take the driver and it's components and we walk it through the EPM process (see <a href="http://rondimon.com/2013/03/21/the-management-operating-system/">here</a> for more details);</li><br />
	<li>When the walk-through is done, we'll have a good set of initiatives that can give new insights and accountability on the main value driver - and use them for competitive advantage.</li><br />
	<li>Then we do it all over again, using a new spin and a new value-driver.</li><br />
</ul><br />
By the end of this session, participants will see a simple process for uncovering new initiatives that use the power of EPM to make a difference in the business.</p>

<p>Hopefully I'll see you at Kscope16 in Chicago and we'll have some fun and learn something new together.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

</Page>
